      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kitsilano takes its name from Squamish chief August Jack Khatsahlano, whose legacy echoes in the neighborhood's streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kitsilano",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Kitsilano Beach mixes sun, sand, and ice cream with a lively street scene, creating a unique blend of bustle and relaxation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.destinationvancouver.com/neighbourhoods/kitsilano",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Kitsilano hosts the longest saltwater pool in North America, a landmark that draws swimmers from across the city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.destinationvancouver.com/neighbourhoods/kitsilano",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "For millennia, the Squamish, Musqueam, and Tsleil‑Waututh peoples shared Kitsilano’s coastal lands for fishing and gathering.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
